{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'dual-aspect' defense fails because it conflates the psychological explanation of why scientists prefer a theory with the epistemological justification for that preference. Popper explicitly rejected evolutionary epistemology as a substitute for logical corroboration, arguing that while selection mechanisms explain the origin of conjectures, they cannot validate the claim that surviving theories are closer to the truth. By reducing the preference for corroborated theories to mere 'survival value' or biological fitness, the target abandons Popper's core commitment to objective knowledge and truth-likeness (verisimilitude), effectively conceding that scientific rationality is nothing more than adaptive behavior, which is the very inductive smuggle Popper sought to avoid.","premise":"Popper distinguished between the context of discovery (psychological/biological) and the context of justification (logical), maintaining that evolutionary success does not equate to logical corroboration or truth approximation.","successor_question":"If the preference for corroborated theories is merely an evolutionary adaptation for survival rather than a rational indicator of truth-likeness, on what grounds can Popper claim science makes objective progress compared to other adaptive belief systems?","target":"e799ae8dcbf57180d8a517149d5f62b6e410385deaed05923499182e028fec2e"}]}